\begin{abstract}
Vision-language-action (VLA) models built on vision-language models (VLMs) inherit strong semantic grounding, but VLM pre-training on high-level tasks such as VQA under-emphasizes low-level spatial cues and physical dynamics that precise manipulation needs. Prediction-based policies show the opposite profile: future-frame pre-training improves spatial grounding but lacks semantic robustness. We present UP-VLA, a single autoregressive model (Phi-1.5 backbone) trained jointly on multi-modal understanding and future image prediction, with unified prompting and attention masks, and an action mechanism that conditions on both a generated scene description and predicted future image tokens. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA reaches an average completed length of 4.08, a 33\% improvement over GR-1 (3.06); on ABCD$\rightarrow$D it reaches 4.42 versus 4.21 for GR-1. On a real Franka Panda robot, ablations show that the understanding objective is critical for unseen-object grasping (0.58 success with, 0.20 without) and that prediction is critical for spatial precision.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Large VLMs are an attractive initialization for robot policies because they bring open-world semantic knowledge to embodied decision making. RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt}, for instance, transfers web knowledge to robotic control, and OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla} and $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi_0} build generalist policies on top of pre-trained VLMs. However, VLMs are predominantly pre-trained on high-level understanding tasks, so they tend to under-emphasize low-level visual and spatial cues---depth, size, relative pose---and physical dynamics. These cues matter for precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes.

A complementary line of work pre-trains policies to predict the future. GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing} uses large-scale video generative pre-training, and video-prediction policies such as SuSIE \citep{black2023zero} and UniPi \citep{du2024learning} plan in image space. Such pre-training improves spatial grounding and physical consistency but, lacking language-scale semantic supervision, generalizes less robustly to semantically new objects and instructions. The two families therefore have opposite strengths (\cref{fig:intro,fig:compare}): VLM-initialized VLAs excel at semantic generalization but struggle with fine-grained control, while prediction-oriented pre-training helps spatial grounding but not semantic robustness. This gap motivates a training paradigm that keeps both high-level multi-modal understanding and low-level future prediction, so that an embodied agent has both conceptual and physical competence.

The key insight of UP-VLA is that these two capabilities can share one autoregressive backbone. Understanding is supervised with image-text data, prediction with robot video and action data, and the action head is conditioned on both a language-grounded description of the scene and an explicit image-space forecast. Because the joint objective enriches the representation with semantic and spatial/dynamics features at once, we expect gains in both simulation and real-world manipulation, which we test below.

