\section{Related Works}
\paragraph{VLM-based robot policies.} Early language-conditioned manipulation used dedicated encoders, e.g. CLIPort \citep{shridhar2022cliport}, and RT-1 \citep{brohan2022rt} scaled a transformer policy to real-world control. Subsequent work initializes policies from VLMs: PaLM-E \citep{driess2023palm} is an embodied multimodal language model, RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt} co-trains on web and robot data, RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision} adapts vision-language foundation models into imitators, and OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla} and $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi_0} offer open and flow-based generalist VLAs. Others add structure, such as language action hierarchies in RT-H \citep{belkhale2024rt} or visual trace prompting in TraceVLA \citep{zheng2024tracevla}. These inherit VLM semantics but not an explicit model of dynamics; the pre-training of VLMs emphasizes high-level tasks such as VQA, and works on spatial reasoning in VLMs \citep{chen2024spatialvlm} reflect that low-level spatial cues are a recognized weakness. UP-VLA adds a future prediction objective on the same backbone, so that spatial and dynamics cues are learned rather than assumed.

\paragraph{Prediction-based policies.} Predicting future observations supplies dense spatial and dynamics supervision. GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing} pre-trains on video generation for manipulation; SuSIE \citep{black2023zero} uses an image-editing diffusion model to generate subgoals; UniPi \citep{du2024learning} casts policy learning as text-guided video generation; PAD \citep{guo2024prediction} jointly denoises future images and actions, and 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d} adds a 3D generative world model. These models improve spatial grounding and physical consistency, yet they lack large-scale language-grounded understanding training and so are weaker at semantic generalization, for example to unseen objects. UP-VLA retains understanding through MMU co-training on LLaVA data and, unlike a pure video-prediction pre-training, conditions its actions on a generated scene description as well as predicted future tokens.

\paragraph{Diffusion and 3D policies.} Diffusion Policy \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy} and 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d} model actions with diffusion, the latter with 3D scene representations, and are strong CALVIN baselines. UP-VLA instead uses an autoregressive LLM with a lightweight action head.

\paragraph{Unified understanding and generation.} Autoregressive and unified models handle image generation and comprehension together, e.g. Show-o \citep{xie2024show}, SEED-X \citep{ge2024seed}, DreamLLM \citep{dong2023dreamllm}, and VideoPoet for video \citep{kondratyuk2023videopoet}; discrete image tokens in the style of VQGAN \citep{esser2021taming} make such autoregressive generation possible. Those models target image or video generation and question answering. We bring the unified recipe to embodied control, where the added challenge is that two different visual representations (continuous CLIP features for understanding, discrete VQ tokens for prediction) must be combined in one LLM, and that actions must be generated conditioned on both streams.

\section{Preliminaries}
We consider language-conditioned manipulation: given a current observation $O_t$ and instruction $L$, a policy outputs a chunk of low-level actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. UP-VLA uses two image pathways. A continuous encoder (CLIP-ViT, \citealp{radford2021learning}) with an MLP projection $E_1$ maps an image to continuous tokens $u$ in the language embedding space, used for MMU. A discrete VQ-GAN tokenizer $E_2$ \citep{esser2021taming} maps an image to codebook indices $v$; future prediction is performed on these indices and a VQ decoder reconstructs images. The language backbone is Phi-1.5 \citep{li2023textbooks}. All encoders are frozen and the LLM is fully fine-tuned. The two pathways are deliberately different: continuous CLIP features carry the semantic content that understanding needs, while discrete tokens turn future prediction into a classification problem over codebook indices, trained with cross-entropy, that a language model handles natively. The model $\pi_\theta$ is therefore a single network that is prompted into three behaviours: describing an image, forecasting a future image, and producing actions.

\section{Methodology}\label{sec:method}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig3_v3.png}
\caption{Overview of UP-VLA.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}
\paragraph{Unified prompting and attention masks.} \Cref{fig:overview,fig:mask} show the model. Each task has its own prompt and mask. For MMU the input is $\{$[MMU], image tokens $u$, language tokens $l\}$; images precede text so image tokens attend to each other, and the model predicts next language tokens, $\hat{L}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(I,L)$. For PRE the input is $\{$[PRE], language tokens $l$, current image tokens $v_t\}$; image tokens come after the text so they attend to all prior tokens, and future image tokens are predicted at aligned positions rather than as a next-token shift, $\hat{O}_{t+\Delta t}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L)$. The distinct masks are needed because the two tasks have different dependency structure: understanding is a standard next-token problem over text conditioned on the image, whereas prediction should let every future image token see the full instruction and the whole current image. For action learning the MMU-derived scene description and current visual tokens are concatenated with the instruction, so actions can attend to both semantic and predictive tokens.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig4.png}
\caption{Illustration of the unified prompting and attention mechanism.}
\label{fig:mask}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Joint prediction and understanding for action.} Given $(O_t,L)$, the model first generates a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(O_t,L_{\mathrm{prompt}})$ with a prompt such as ``describe this image'', embeds additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$, and forms the extended instruction $L'=[E_1(O'_t),s,L]$. It then jointly predicts future image tokens and actions, $(\hat{O}_{t+\Delta t},\hat{A}_{t:t+\Delta t})=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L')$. Conditioning on $s$ lets the action tokens use a language-grounded reading of the scene (objects and approximate relative positions), while the predicted future tokens expose the expected dynamics. A lightweight policy head (single-layer attention pooling, MAP, followed by an MLP) maps the action tokens to continuous actions, $\hat a_{t:t+\Delta t}=\mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{MAP}(\hat A_{t:t+\Delta t}))$.

\paragraph{Objectives.} The MMU loss is the language-modeling cross-entropy $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}=-\sum_i\log p_\theta(l_i\mid u,l_{1:i-1})$. The prediction loss is the cross-entropy over future image tokens, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}=-\sum_{j=1}^{M}\log p_\theta(v'_j\mid l,v_{1:M})$, where $v'$ are the tokens of the future image. The action loss combines regression on the position and a binary cross-entropy on the gripper-end signal, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}=\|\hat a_{\mathrm{pos}}-a_{\mathrm{pos}}\|_2^2+\mathrm{BCE}(\hat a_{\mathrm{end}},a_{\mathrm{end}})$. The total loss is $\mathcal{L}=\lambda_1\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}$ (the overview does not report the $\lambda$ values).

\paragraph{Training and inference.} Stage 1 pre-trains on a mixture of MMU data (LLaVA-665k, \citealp{liu2024visual}) and PRE data (25k Bridge demonstrations, \citealp{walke2023bridgedata}) with task-specific prompts and masks. Stage 2 fine-tunes for actions on downstream robot data with joint prompting, continuing MMU co-training to retain semantic capability. At test time the model sees $O_t$ and $L$, generates $s$, forms $L'$, runs the PRE pathway to obtain future tokens and action tokens, decodes actions with MAP+MLP, executes them, and repeats.

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:exp}
\paragraph{Setup.} In simulation we use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} with ABC$\rightarrow$D (train on scenes A, B, C; test on D) and ABCD$\rightarrow$D, reporting the success rate of completing 1--5 chained tasks and the average length. In the real world we use a Franka Emika Panda with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills, and evaluate seen tasks, unseen-object grasping, and precise operations such as cable routing and small-object grasping, with 20 trials per task (\cref{fig:env}). Baselines are VLA-based (RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision}, 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d}, RT-1 \citep{brohan2022rt}, and our RT-2-style variant UP-VLA-RT-2), and prediction-based (GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing}, SuSIE \citep{black2023zero}, UniPi \citep{du2024learning}, 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d}, Diffusion Policy \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy}), plus UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu, a GR-1-like model with our backbone. Starred entries were reproduced by us; others are copied from the original papers. The metric is the average number of tasks completed in a row out of five chained instructions, the standard long-horizon CALVIN measure. The model is evaluated zero-shot on the target scene, so ABC$\rightarrow$D tests generalization to a new environment. Optimizer settings, the $\lambda$ weights and other hyperparameters are not reported in the material available to us.

