\paragraph{Ablations.} \Cref{table:ablate} removes one component at a time. Removing prediction collapses ABC$\rightarrow$D length from 4.08 to 1.44 and real-world success to 0.65 (seen) and 0.35 (unseen), so prediction is crucial for spatial and dynamics grounding. Removing Bridge pre-training lowers length to 2.74, so predictive pre-training on 25k demonstrations transfers. Removing MMU barely changes simulation (3.89) or seen tasks (0.85) but cuts unseen-object success from 0.58 to 0.20, and removing only the MMU conditioning of actions gives 3.99 / 0.80 / 0.50: understanding matters most for semantic generalization, and conditioning actions on the generated description adds to it. The two settings of the ablation disagree in informative ways: in simulation, where objects are drawn from the training distribution, removing MMU costs only 0.19 in average length, whereas on unseen real-world objects the cost is 0.38 in success rate (0.58 to 0.20); the benefit of understanding is thus concentrated where semantics are needed. Conversely, removing prediction hurts every column, including seen real-world tasks (0.80 to 0.65), which matches its role in spatial and dynamics grounding. Single-run numbers are reported; no variance across seeds is given in the material, so small differences, such as the 0.85 versus 0.80 on seen tasks without MMU, should not be over-read.

