\paragraph{Discussion of baselines.} The baselines span the design space the paper targets (\cref{fig:compare}). VLA-based models (RoboFlamingo, 3D-VLA, UP-VLA-RT-2) rely on a VLM initialization and train an action head on robot data; prediction-based models (GR-1, SuSIE, UniPi) use future-image or video generation as the learning signal; 3D Diffuser Actor and Diffusion Policy are non-LLM policies. In \cref{table1}, the best baseline average length is 3.35 (3D Diffuser Actor), which UP-VLA exceeds by 0.73, and the best LLM-based or prediction-based baseline is GR-1 at 3.06. The VLA-only variant UP-VLA-RT-2 reaches only 1.44, equal to the ablation without prediction in \cref{table:ablate}; the two numbers coincide, which suggests that a VLM-initialized policy without future prediction is far from sufficient on CALVIN. In contrast, the prediction-only variant UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu (3.13) is competitive with GR-1 but falls 0.95 short of the full model, so neither ingredient alone explains the result; their combination does.

\paragraph{Per-step behaviour.} The advantage of UP-VLA grows with the chain length. On ABC$\rightarrow$D the first-task success is 0.928 (3D Diffuser Actor: 0.938), but by the fifth task UP-VLA retains 0.699 against 0.412 for 3D Diffuser Actor and 0.401 for GR-1. Long-horizon performance depends on errors not compounding, which is consistent with the model having both an accurate semantic reading of the scene and an explicit forecast of the consequence of its actions. On ABCD$\rightarrow$D (\cref{table2}) the same holds: UP-VLA's success on the fifth task is 0.812 versus 0.731 for GR-1 and 0.660 for RoboFlamingo, while its first-task rate (0.962) is slightly below RoboFlamingo's 0.964. We do not have an experiment that isolates the mechanism of this error-compounding effect, so it is an interpretation rather than a measured result.

\section{Limitations}
Predicted images show background color shifts across scenes, indicating insufficient visual-generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. MMU occasionally misclassifies objects, reflecting backbone and data scale; larger VLMs and richer data could reduce this. Joint tasks add training cost and sequence length, which parameter-efficient fine-tuning or structured memory might reduce. Finally, the model uses 2D images only; 3D inputs, multi-view or explicit physics modeling are untested. Our results also come from one benchmark family and one real robot.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future visual prediction in one autoregressive VLA and conditions actions on scene descriptions and predicted futures. It reaches 4.08 average length on CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D (3.06 for GR-1) and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D, and ablations attribute spatial gains to prediction and semantic generalization to understanding. The evidence is limited to the reported benchmarks and robot, without seed variance, and scaling the backbone, data and generative quality remains future work.

